## What it actually does

```jsx
'use client';   // must be the very first line, before imports

import { useState } from 'react';

export function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>{count}</button>;
}
```

It does not mean "this component runs only on the client" — client components are still server-rendered for the initial HTML. It means **"from here down, the code is included in the client bundle and hydrated."**

---

## Why placement matters so much

```jsx
// ❌ 'use client' on the layout — the entire app is now a client app
'use client';
export default function Layout({ children }) {
  const [sidebarOpen, setSidebarOpen] = useState(false);
  return <div>{children}</div>;
}
```

Every component imported by this file, and everything they import, ships to the browser. You have re-created a standard SPA and kept none of the benefit.

```jsx
// ✅ Push it to the leaf
// layout.tsx — stays a Server Component
export default function Layout({ children }) {
  return <div><SidebarToggle /><main>{children}</main></div>;
}

// SidebarToggle.tsx
'use client';
export function SidebarToggle() {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  return <button onClick={() => setOpen(o => !o)}>Menu</button>;
}
```

Only the toggle's code crosses the network.

---

## Passing server content through a client component

A Client Component cannot import a Server Component, but `children` works because the element is created on the server:

```jsx
// Server Component
<ClientAccordion>
  <ExpensiveServerRenderedContent />   {/* rendered on the server */}
</ClientAccordion>
```

This is the key technique for keeping interactive wrappers cheap.

---

## Props must be serialisable

```jsx
// ❌ Functions cannot cross the boundary
<ClientThing onSave={() => db.save()} />

// ✅ Server Actions are the exception — they serialise to a reference
<ClientThing action={saveAction} />
```

Allowed: primitives, plain objects and arrays, Dates, Maps, Sets, JSX elements, and Server Actions. Not allowed: ordinary functions, class instances, symbols.

---

## Its counterpart

`'use server'` marks a **Server Action** — a function that can be called from the client but executes on the server. The two directives are often confused; `'use client'` marks a bundling boundary, `'use server'` marks a callable server function.

## The shape

```jsx
// actions.ts
'use server';

import { z } from 'zod';
import { auth } from './auth';

const schema = z.object({ title: z.string().min(1).max(200) });

export async function createPost(formData) {
  const session = await auth();                     // ← authenticate
  if (!session) throw new Error('Unauthorized');     // ← authorise

  const { title } = schema.parse({                   // ← validate
    title: formData.get('title'),
  });

  await db.post.create({ data: { title, authorId: session.userId } });
  revalidatePath('/posts');
}
```

```jsx
// A form that works before JavaScript loads
<form action={createPost}>
  <input name="title" />
  <button>Create</button>
</form>
```

No `onSubmit`, no `preventDefault`, no `fetch`, no API route.

---

## Progressive enhancement

Because it is a real form `action`, submission works with JavaScript disabled or still loading. Once React hydrates, it upgrades to a client-side call without a full page reload. That is a genuine capability the `onSubmit` + `fetch` pattern cannot match.

---

## With pending state and errors

```jsx
import { useActionState } from 'react';

function PostForm() {
  const [state, formAction, isPending] = useActionState(async (prev, formData) => {
    try {
      await createPost(formData);
      return { ok: true };
    } catch (e) {
      return { error: e.message };
    }
  }, null);

  return (
    <form action={formAction}>
      <input name="title" />
      <button disabled={isPending}>{isPending ? 'Saving…' : 'Create'}</button>
      {state?.error && <p role="alert">{state.error}</p>}
    </form>
  );
}
```

`useFormStatus` gives a nested button the parent form's pending state without prop drilling.

---

## The security point — say this unprompted

> **A Server Action is a public HTTP endpoint.** The bundler generates an id for it and anyone can invoke it directly with a crafted request.

So every action must:

1. **Authenticate** — who is calling?
2. **Authorise** — are they allowed to do this to *this* record?
3. **Validate** — never trust the payload shape

Not being reachable from your UI is not protection. Interviewers ask this specifically to see whether you treat actions as function calls or as endpoints.

---

## When not to use one

They are for mutations. For reading data, fetch directly in a Server Component — an action adds a round trip for nothing.
